WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.708 --> 00:00:06.080
Speaker 1: Second piece of hugely positive news&nbsp;
and I'm not. You know what, before you even get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.680
into this, let me go ahead and say, because&nbsp;
this is positive news all over social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.240
people are glad about it, but saying, go&nbsp;
further and I'm going to talk about that. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.280
the piece of positive news&nbsp;
that I want to talk about now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.160
is that the Biden administration announced&nbsp;
on Wednesday they were going to extend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:34.960
The student loan debt repayment deadline, meaning&nbsp;
instead of payments resuming on February 1st,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.760
they are now set to resume on May 1st. They have&nbsp;
kicked it back 90 days, giving people more time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.840
I guess, to make adjustments. Whatever it is,&nbsp;
those three months mean the world to people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:55.360
suffering under the burden of student loan&nbsp;
debt. And this wouldn't have happened. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.320
this is really the positive part of the story.&nbsp;
This wouldn't have happened without the push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.680
from the public for the administration to do&nbsp;
this. That's the real positive part of this story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.280
that the activism work, much like actually&nbsp;
with the COVID tests in a week and a half ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.960
Jen Psaki kind of laughed it off during a press&nbsp;
conference of What are we supposed to send a test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.240
to everybody, you know, give it to them for free&nbsp;
and then after pushback to like, OK, we're we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.720
actually we are going to do that same thing with&nbsp;
this student loan repayment. People have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.120
pushing them, warning them. Even economists&nbsp;
have been warning them, you're about to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.840
a major blow to the economy, taking out roughly&nbsp;
$85 billion from it when these payments restart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.800
Is that where you want to be in an election year?&nbsp;
Tanking your own economy, putting hardship on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.840
voters and the administration says, OK, we're&nbsp;
going to kick the can down the road 90 days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.960
So now it comes to the other&nbsp;
part of it, right? Cancelation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.520
full blown, absolute cancelation. And of course,&nbsp;
that is what a lot of people are pushing for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.840
To be honest, that is what I'm pushing for. That&nbsp;
is what I want to see happen in this country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.240
That's what needs to happen. Yes, I understand&nbsp;
all the arguments on the other side, you took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.280
out the loans you're responsible for repaying and&nbsp;
what do we do next? Repay it, pay off your house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.800
Well, yeah, you want to get people into&nbsp;
homes, you want to get them off the streets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.400
You want to give them the opportunity&nbsp;
for a real chance at a better life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:36.000
Yeah, you could. You should. So I am all&nbsp;
for 100 percent full blown student loan debt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.960
cancelation short of that, I'm in favor&nbsp;
of Biden keeping his campaign promise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:46.000
which is to pay off $10000 of everybody's&nbsp;
loans. He did make that promise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.760
It's still on his Twitter. You can go and see it&nbsp;
yourself. He said he would do that. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.960
we can talk about the economic impacts of&nbsp;
that which, you know, some people, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.320
the only thing that matters. So I went through&nbsp;
compile these statistics, looked at some old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.520
studies from this year earlier this year. Here's&nbsp;
what we have right now. As of today, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.840
one point five seven trillion dollars in student&nbsp;
loan debt held by more than 40 million borrowers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:27.040
The Levy Economic Institute of Bard College used&nbsp;
data from 2016, and they found that cancelation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:33.440
the outstanding student loan debt would translate&nbsp;
to an increase of 86 billion to $108 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.920
on average per year to our gross domestic&nbsp;
product here in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.720
Information from just a couple of&nbsp;
months ago. This is actually from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:52.960
the Federal Reserve says that those payments&nbsp;
are averaging two hundred to $300 per month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.960
That's the average some people&nbsp;
pay less. Some people pay more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.960
But that's what we're looking at. So a full&nbsp;
cancelation, not just 10000, although 10000, would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.720
cancel debt for millions of people. You're talking&nbsp;
about giving them an extra two to $300 per month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.240
which is what they've had during the pandemic. For&nbsp;
a lot of people, that's like an extra paycheck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:25.520
And do you know what people do with that money?&nbsp;
Well, we know because the analysis also came out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.120
this week, along with the child tax credit,&nbsp;
they're using it to pay off other debts. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.280
people are actually able to put some of it in&nbsp;
savings. They're using it to pay medical bills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.160
They're using it to pay housing expenses. They're&nbsp;
using it to buy food. They're using it on car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:47.200
repairs, which is something that a lot of people&nbsp;
tend to overlook as a major factor. You know, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.880
we talk about those studies that show that people&nbsp;
can't afford an emergency of $700 or more, a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.240
of those emergencies either involve in a mere and&nbsp;
immediate health crisis or an automobile crisis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.520
Car repairs are not cheap. And the cost of all&nbsp;
of those continue to go up each and every year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.400
and that becomes a major factor that takes&nbsp;
away somebody's ability to get to and from work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.240
to take their kids to and from&nbsp;
school or daycare or whatever it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.640
So that is a major problem. So two&nbsp;
to three hundred dollars a month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.720
If we had full student loan debt, forgiveness&nbsp;
would absolutely be life changing for these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:31.760
individuals. And we have seen that since March&nbsp;
of 2020, when these payments were first paused.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:40.080
So I applaud the administration for listening&nbsp;
to the public and pushing this 90 days back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:48.560
Personally, I want it all canceled.&nbsp;
Economically, it should be all canceled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.440
Zero it out. Change people's lives for the&nbsp;
better. And of course, the other side of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.840
that is certainly would certainly help&nbsp;
Democrats politically in the midterms. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.640
we got to take a quick break. I'm Farron Cousins&nbsp;
sitting in for David Pakman. You can find me all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.680
over social media at fair and balanced. We'll be&nbsp;
right back with more of the David Pakman show.

